reac-label queries a Roland V-Mixer / M-5000 over its remote-control protocol
and builds a REAC-slot -> channel-name table for reac-aes67.

## Third-party code and references

reac-label is an original, stdlib-only Python implementation. It contains no
code copied from any upstream project. The items below are credited because
they grounded the protocol parsing or supplied factual data.

### Code

None. All source files in this repository are independently authored. In
particular, `reaclabel/proto.py` (protocol framing and reply parsing) and
`reaclabel/simulator.py` (a TCP query simulator) are original implementations
of a documented wire format and copy no upstream code.

### Reference data used to ground tests and parsing

- JamesCC/VMXProxyPy -- LGPL-3.0 / GPL-3.0, Copyright (C) JamesCC.
  Its `simrc.txt` sample command/response pairs (e.g. `CNS:I1,"NoName";`,
  `PIS:I22,RAI22;`) were used as *data* to ground the protocol-parsing tests
  and the simulator's reply framing in `reaclabel/proto.py` and
  `reaclabel/simulator.py`. No VMXProxyPy code is included or derived.
- bitfocus/companion-module-roland-m5000 -- MIT, Copyright (c) the
  companion-module-roland-m5000 contributors. Per-model channel counts (factual
  data only) informed the `--model` input defaults. No code is included or
  derived.

### Protocol and standard facts referenced (no copyright claimed)

The following document only the facts of a protocol or specification; reac-label
references those facts and claims no copyright over them:

- Roland V-Mixer / M-5000 RS-232C / Telnet remote-control reference
  (documentation (C) Roland Corporation) -- the command/reply wire format
  (3-letter CODE + `:` + CSV args + `;`, STX-prefixed serial form vs. telnet
  `OK` ack, `ERR:<n>;` errors) implemented in `reaclabel/proto.py` and answered
  by `reaclabel/simulator.py`. Only the documented protocol facts are used; no
  Roland code or text is included.
